## Required per-workflow ledger

The [workflow ledger](../architecture/workflows.md) expands these families into
66 operation/workflow classes. C01 records machine-readable evidence rows and
consuming chunks map exact supported operations/options before implementation.
Every row needs an ID, baseline source, current classification, responsible chunk,
exact SDK/shared dependency contract, and independent expectations. Preserve four
evidence categories separately: source implementation, fixture/contract results,
native/live qualification, and final installed-artifact qualification. Record
denied/unsupported/partial results honestly rather than collapsing them to pass.

G01/C01 adds a live_requirement (required/not_required with reason) and separate
ready/blocked-by-access status to every row under [live-checks.md](live-checks.md).
The owner approves the expanded list before C25. Missing access cannot shrink
mandatory scope; a reduction requires a recorded owner decision and new review.
T23 cross-feature offline acceptance applies to local work and pending operations,
not just Academy. C24 closes that cumulative matrix.

V2 source completeness alone does not close a row. Each workflow needs applicable
success and failure assertions, context/authority coverage, native acceptance and
required live/artifact evidence. C25/C26 review the cumulative ledger against
the final frozen input set. A required blocked check blocks stable completion.

## Intentional non-goals and retained classifications

- Financial desktop billing/monetization and media-generation infrastructure are
  excluded. Remote operational cost/limit administration is retained.
- Exception Patterns remains explicitly synthetic/inert if shown. FP/FN reports
  do not suppress scans or modify policy.
- New Red Team execution and dedicated Model Security execution remain outside
  parity. Their existence in the new SDK does not authorize adding product scope.
- Academy admin/assessor operations remain web-only. Learner feedback with an
  administrative namespace is classified by actual authority, not string matching.
- No browser app, in-app updater, or additional supported OS/architecture is
  required for first stable v2.
- Shared Academy appearance replaces the GPUI presentation implementation. Do
  not infer a pixel clone or identical internal widget/theme catalogue. Preserve
  theme preference workflows and specify reviewed mappings for imported settings.
- The generic SCM workbench catalogue does not prove all 193 requests were live
  tested. Classify its supported execution/admission separately from dedicated
  workflows and bind its request metadata to a reviewed source snapshot.

Managed scan/identity HTTP deliberately defaults to Direct rather than v1's
ambient system proxy behavior. On mandatory-proxy networks, onboarding/settings
shows Direct/System/Explicit policy and requires operator selection; a failed
first request must surface proxy setup rather than an unexplained regression.
Generic workbench remains System by default. C02/C15 qualify all modes.
# 4. Managed HTTP enters native configuration authority

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D014,D016 and C14/C15 requirements.

V1's workbench has real request-document and transport depth. Its redactions-based
redirect guard is not explicit tenant-credential classification, Live mode lacks
application destination binding, and its post-401 retry can resend a mutation.
The stricter v2 review/no-replay policy must be enforced below every Send path.

Keep RequestDocument reconciliation separate. Native execution admission captures
a fresh immutable effective request and explicitly distinguishes tenant-managed,
workbench-owned and anonymous credentials. Known managed reads need capability/
destination admission. Known mutations need exact Configuration review. Unknown
managed methods/paths are denied, even if presented as a read or generic draft.
Ordinary workbench-owned/anonymous HTTP remains supported.

Replace the editable SCM collection-ID credential gate with native-owned
capability registration. Imported IDs/references cannot authorize tenant secret
resolution. An edited token_url/request URL is admitted against the explicit
credential destination before secrets are obtained; an ID change never creates
a capability. Preserve operator-reviewed endpoint configuration separately.

Bind review to expanded URL/method/headers/body and file hashes, variables,
cookies, credential/context/destination and resource classification. Recheck
before dispatch; changed input/context/files require fresh review. Rust constructs
the actual request; renderer fields cannot mark it preapproved.

Managed redirects are disabled initially: a 3xx is shown, and following it is a
new prepared/admitted request. Generic redirects preserve bounded cookie/credential
origin policy. No automatic workbench resend follows HTTP401, refresh, reconnect,
lost response, import or history replay. Refresh can precede a new explicit Send.

The built-in catalogue has 193 request specifications, 81 with mutation methods;
this is metadata parity, not a claim of dedicated/live-qualified product support.
Tests cover imported/edited requests, unknown managed endpoints, direct IPC bypass,
ambiguous paths/auth headers, same/cross-origin redirects, stale cookie/file/
variable/context bindings, double Apply and post-401/lost-response no resend.

This is a stricter v2 product contract, not an allegation that v1 failed its own
contract. See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).
